    "check.missing-module-tag.compares": "Every source file against the `---@module` line it should open with.",
    "check.missing-module-tag.why": "The module name is what every other check and every link resolves against; a file without one is in the map only as a path.",
    "check.missing-readme.compares": "The directories of the tree that should have one against a `README.md` in each.",
    "check.missing-readme.why": "The README is the front door of a directory, and the map links to it from the node; a missing one is a dead end for the reader who starts there.",
    "check.missing-summary.compares": "Every module against the one description line under its `@module` tag.",
    "check.missing-summary.why": "The summary is the text every list, box and search result shows for the module; without it the map names the file and says nothing about it.",
    "check.module-path-mismatch.compares": "The name a file declares in `@module` against the name its path gives it.",
    "check.module-path-mismatch.why": "A declared name that no `require` can reach is documentation of a module that does not exist; it is usually what a move or a rename left behind.",
    "check.orphaned-class-alias.compares": "Every declared `@class` and `@alias` against the type references in the tree.",
    "check.orphaned-class-alias.why": "A type nothing refers to is usually what a refactoring left behind, and it keeps describing a shape no value has any more.",
    "check.param-name-mismatch.compares": "The name of each `@param` line against the parameter at the same position of the signature.",
    "check.param-name-mismatch.why": "The usual cause is a parameter renamed in the code and not in the comment; the documentation then describes an argument that is not there.",
    "check.placeholder-readme-link.compares": "Every link target in a README against the `${VAR}` placeholder form.",
    "check.placeholder-readme-link.why": "No Markdown viewer expands a placeholder, so such a link works in nobody's browser, whatever the variable is set to.",
    "check.require-cycle.compares": "The require edges of the tree against a loop among them.",
    "check.require-cycle.why": "Modules that require each other load in an order nobody chose, and one of them sees the other half-built.",
    "check.require-not-declared.compares": "Every require of a name under this project's own prefix against the modules the tree declares.",
    "check.require-not-declared.why": "Such a require can only fail at run time: the name belongs to this tree and no file answers to it.",
    "check.sibling-reference-missing.compares": "References into a sibling repository against that repository's checkout.",
    "check.sibling-reference-missing.why": "A path into another repository breaks without a trace when that repository moves the file; nothing in this tree changes when it happens.",
    "check.tag-file-unavailable.compares": "Every configured `tag_files` entry against a readable `module_map.json` at that place.",
    "check.tag-file-unavailable.why": "Without the other project's map the requires into it were not checked at all, and a check that did not run must not read as one that passed.",
    "check.tag-require-missing.compares": "The requires into another mapped project (`tag_files`) against the modules that project's own map declares.",
    "check.tag-require-missing.why": "A require of a module the other project no longer has fails at run time, in a file this tree's own checks would call clean.",
    "check.test-references-missing.compares": "The module members a spec file reaches for against what the module really has.",
    "check.test-references-missing.why": "A spec that calls a function which is gone fails late, or never runs that part at all; either way it no longer tests what its name says.",
    "check.tools-spec-invalid.compares": "Each entry of the tools manifest against the shape the manifest must have.",
